    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d766">

                <pb facs="1490" n="1468" sameAs="frus:frus1946v01/pg1468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.796/2–446: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chairman of the United States Delegation</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Baker</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hamilton</hi>
                        </placeName> [<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1946-02-04T16:45:00-05:00"
                            notBefore="1946-02-03T00:00:00-04:00">undated</date>.]<lb/>[Received
                        February 4, 1946—4:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>For Clayton from Baker, copy for Walstrom; copy for Hickerson. Last remaining
                    questions of conference worked out today. Additions to final act, bilateral air
                    transport agreement and annex thereto except for Article III in annex were
                    teletyped to you this noon.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d766-f1"/> Ill in annex is
                    given at end of this message. You have already received and have approved II of
                    annex, draft of air transport agreement and draft of final act of
                        conference.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d766-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>General Kuter instructed Colonel Tate who returned to Washington Friday with
                    final draft of bases agreement not to formally submit that draft to Joint Chiefs
                    of Staff until advised from here that British were going to approve it although
                    he was instructed to thoroughly brief advisers to Joint Chiefs of Staff. This is
                    to avoid embarrassment of having JCS twice<anchor n="38" xml:id="d766-f3"/>
                    approve bases agreement only to have British ask more changes. I would
                    appreciate your approval of final material in prior teletype of this date, of
                    the material in this teletype and of the bases agreement—the latter subject of
                    course to <pb facs="1491" n="1469" xml:id="pg1469"/> receiving subsequent Joint
                    Chiefs of Staff approval. I would hope all clearances could be obtained from
                    London and Washington by Tuesday or Wednesday.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d766-f4"/>
                    Meanwhile full delegation will stand by here in case British Government should
                    give only qualified approval. I belief Self is very hopeful that he will get
                    full approval but the overall agreement would seem to be much harder for the
                    British to swallow than for us. If London approval is given it will be over the
                    strong protest of some government officials. You will know best whether to use
                    your influence with Halifax.</p>
                <p>[Here follows draft of Part III of the Annex, regarding route descriptions.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Baker</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d766-f1" n="36" xml:id="d766-f1-content">
                <p>This refers apparently to the undated telegram printed <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d766-f2" n="37" xml:id="d766-f2-content">
                <p>See footnote 17, <ref target="#pg1459">p. 1459</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d766-f3" n="38" xml:id="d766-f3-content">
                <p>The Joint Chiefs had already on January 25 approved the draft they then had in
                    hand (U.S. Delegation Document USD–14); see footnote 16, <ref target="#pg1458"
                        >p. 1458</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d766-f4" n="39" xml:id="d766-f4-content">
                <p>That is, February 5 or 6.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
